1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,300
Three shorts, one job. We write an event on the books. First the equation, then
the journal line, then the clock we date it with.

2
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:18,120
By the end you should keep the two sides equal, write the line, and date it to the
work — not only to the cash.

3
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:29,380
This sitting starts with the books. Every resource the firm controls has a claim on
it. The equation is the test that those two sides still match.

4
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:35,311
Assets are resources the business
controls. Liabilities are claims by
outsiders.

5
00:00:35,331 --> 00:00:42,560
Equity is the owners’ residual claim. The
two sides must be equal after every event.

6
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:46,829
The firm borrows cash from a bank. Cash,
an asset, rises.

7
00:00:46,849 --> 00:00:51,810
The bank loan, a liability, rises by the
same amount. Equity does not move.

8
00:00:51,830 --> 00:00:56,790
The equation still holds.

9
00:00:56,810 --> 00:00:59,763
After each event,

10
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:03,231
name the asset that moved and the claim
that moved with it.

11
00:01:03,251 --> 00:01:06,700
Next we write that same event as a debit
and a credit.

12
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:16,830
The equation said both sides must move together. Debit and credit are only the
two sides of that one line. Neither word means good or bad.

13
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:21,366
A debit is the left side of an account. A
credit is the right side.

14
00:01:21,386 --> 00:01:26,910
Every journal entry needs at least one of
each, and the totals must match.

15
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:30,516
Buying supplies with cash increases an
asset on the left and decreases another
asset on the right. Debit supplies.

16
00:01:30,536 --> 00:01:34,723
Credit cash.

17
00:01:34,743 --> 00:01:38,930
The line still balances.

18
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:43,646
Do not memorise “debit means increase.

19
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:49,148
” Name the account on the left and the
account on the right, then check the
amounts.

20
00:01:49,168 --> 00:01:54,650
Next we ask when that line is dated — when
cash moves, or when the work is done.

21
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:08,360
We can now write a balanced line. The remaining question is the date. Cash in the
till is easy to see. Profit uses a different clock — the work, not the wallet.

22
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:13,220
Under the accrual basis, revenue is
recorded when it is earned and expense
when it is incurred.

23
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:19,160
Under the cash basis, both wait until
money changes hands.

24
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:23,387
A tutor finishes a course in June. The
family pays in July.

25
00:02:23,407 --> 00:02:28,319
Accrual books record the fee in June. Cash
books stay silent until July.

26
00:02:28,338 --> 00:02:33,250
The work was June.

27
00:02:33,270 --> 00:02:38,803
Write the rule before the example. Accrual
follows the work. Cash follows the wallet.

28
00:02:38,823 --> 00:02:45,590
Both can be true books. They answer
different questions.

29
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:49,328
First ask whether the service is finished.
Then ask whether money moved.

30
00:02:49,348 --> 00:02:53,689
Then pick accrual or cash for that report.

31
00:02:53,709 --> 00:02:58,050
Do not mix the two clocks in one total.

32
00:02:58,070 --> 00:03:01,881
If the report is meant to show
performance, use the work clock.

33
00:03:01,901 --> 00:03:06,350
This sitting ends here: we can record an
event and date it.

34
00:03:06,370 --> 00:03:10,820
The next sitting asks which period the
amount belongs to.

35
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:17,840
You can state the equation, place a debit and a credit, and choose the work clock
over the cash clock.

36
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:26,020
That is the books unit. Next sitting: which period gets the sale, the matching cost,
and the machine.
